\details{
\strong{Single-variable mode} (when \code{x} resolves to exactly one variable):
The focal variable name becomes the first column. Rows follow the factor
level order (if the variable is a factor) or ascending sort order otherwise.

\strong{Multi-variable mode} (when \code{x} resolves to two or more variables):
Results are stacked in long format. The \code{names_to} column contains the
variable label (when \code{label_vars = TRUE}) or the raw variable name as
fallback. The \code{values_to} column contains the response values.

\strong{Domain estimation:} Proportions use the ratio linearization approach,
equivalent to \code{survey::svymean()} on a binary indicator within the active
domain. The full design structure is used for variance estimation — rows are
not physically removed for domain/group subsets.

\strong{\code{na.rm = FALSE}:} \code{NA} is appended as the last level. All proportions
(including non-\code{NA} levels) have their denominator inflated to include
\code{NA} rows, so the \code{pct} column sums to 1.
